  │ or full                   │                                                                                     │
  ├───────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Stored JSON corrupt       │ [38;5;153mgetSession()[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m — reads as logged out                                     │
  └───────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  The rule underneath the table: [1mthe store is a cache, and the server is the source of truth.[0m Every consumer must treat
  [38;5;153mgetSession()[39m returning a value as "probably this person" and not as proof of authorization. Nothing in it is a
  credential.

  [1mOne lifecycle gap I want to flag.[0m [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m survives browser restart, and right now nothing in the app ever clears
  it. As designed so far, the first person to log in stays "logged in" on that browser forever — there's no logout
  control in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, so [38;5;153mclearSession()[39m would ship with no caller. That's both dead code and, on a shared machine, a
  real defect rather than a missing nicety.

  There's a related problem: with no UI reading the session, the whole feature is invisible. You couldn't confirm by
  hand that any of this works.

  A thin consumer solves both at once — a line reading [38;5;153mSigned in as <username>[39m rendered on load from [38;5;153mgetSession()[39m, plus
  a logout button calling [38;5;153mclearSession()[39m. It's maybe fifteen lines, it gives [38;5;153mclearSession()[39m a caller, it makes the
  feature verifiable without a debugger, and it's the first instance of the "other forms read the identity" pattern the
  rest of the app will copy.
